Task 1: Configure Centralized Authentication
Overview
Implement FreeRADIUS as a centralized authentication system for network device access control.

Step 1: Configure FreeRADIUS Server
Install and start the RADIUS service:

# Update and install FreeRADIUS
sudo apt update
sudo apt install freeradius freeradius-utils -y

# Start the service
sudo systemctl start freeradius
sudo systemctl enable freeradius
Step 2: Define RADIUS Clients
Edit the clients configuration:

sudo nano /etc/freeradius/3.0/clients.conf
Add this configuration at the end:

client router-lab {
    ipaddr = 127.0.0.1
    secret = Lab2SecureKey2024
    require_message_authenticator = no
    nas_type = cisco
}
Step 3: Create User Accounts
Edit the users file:

sudo nano /etc/freeradius/3.0/users
Add these user definitions at the top:

netadmin Cleartext-Password := "SecureAdmin123!"
    Service-Type = Administrative-User,
    Cisco-AVPair = "shell:priv-lvl=15"

netoper Cleartext-Password := "OperPass456!"
    Service-Type = NAS-Prompt-User,
    Cisco-AVPair = "shell:priv-lvl=1"

secanalyst Cleartext-Password := "AnalystKey789!"
    Service-Type = Administrative-User,
    Cisco-AVPair = "shell:priv-lvl=10"
Step 4: Test Authentication
Restart FreeRADIUS and test user authentication:

# Restart service
sudo systemctl restart freeradius

# Test authentication for each user
radtest netadmin "SecureAdmin123!" 127.0.0.1 1812 Lab2SecureKey2024
radtest netoper "OperPass456!" 127.0.0.1 1812 Lab2SecureKey2024
radtest secanalyst "AnalystKey789!" 127.0.0.1 1812 Lab2SecureKey2024

# Test invalid credentials
radtest invaliduser "wrongpass" 127.0.0.1 1812 Lab2SecureKey2024
